Skip next section Opposition creating 'atmosphere of anarchy,' says Modi's party 10/08/2026 October 8, 2026 Opposition creating 'atmosphere of anarchy,' says Modi's party
Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi's Bharatiya Janata Party on Thursday slammed protesting opposition leaders for "creating an atmosphere of anarchy" and "undermining the constitution," according to Press Trust of India.&#160;
This comes as hundreds of opposition lawmakers including Modi's arguably biggest challenger, Rahul Gandhi, protests in New Delhi over 'vote theft' and demands the resignation of India's Chief Election Commissioner (CEC) Gyanesh Kumar.&#160;
"It would not be wrong to say that all the allegations being levelled by Rahul Gandhi, Arvind Kejriwal and various Opposition parties, that democracy is being destroyed, raise the question: what is the basis for these allegations?" BJP National Spokesperson Gaurav Bhatia said at a press conference in Delhi.
"These are baseless allegations being leveled by an irresponsible opposition. The claim that democracy is being destroyed and that the constitution is being undermined has no basis and not an iota of evidence," he told reporters.&#160;
Indian opposition leaders are scheduled to protest in central Delhi today for the third straight day, without police permission. As per Indian media, Indian National Congress (INC) President Mallikarjun Kharge is hosting protesting MPs at a well-known eatery not far from the designated protest site of Jantar Mantar. They will likely protest after lunch.&#160;
On Wednesday, the lawmakers met at a different eatery followed by a march towards the Election Commission of India (ECI).&#160;
Skip next section Veteran actor Nana Patekar dies at 75 10/08/2026 October 8, 2026 Veteran actor Nana Patekar dies at 75
Nana Patekar, a well-known Indian actor and filmmaker, died on Thursday after what Indian media reported was a severe heart attack. He was 75.&#160;
Patekar passed away at his home in the scenic state of Goa but was later taken to a hospital where doctors pronounced him dead, news agency Press Trust of India reported.
It said Patekar was found unconscious in his apartment and could not be revived despite 45 minutes of cardiopulmonary resuscitation. Authorities will conduct postmortem procedures to ascertain the cause of the death, the report said.
Born in 1951 in the western state of Maharashtra, Patekar built his career over five decades, largely in Hindi and Marathi-language cinema. He was known for his intense, often complex roles, punctuated with&#160;unconventional comedy.&#160;
Patekar was a well-known figure in both Hindi and Marathi language cinema in India Image: Prodip Guha/Hindustan Times/Newscom World/IMAGO
In his early work, he earned acclaim for movies like "Parinda"&#160;where he played a ruthless crime lord&#160;in the&#160;menacing setting of the underworld-dominant Mumbai. One of his better-loved movies includes "Khamoshi"&#160;— a musical in which Patekar portrays Joseph, a loving yet stubborn deaf and mute father to the lead actress.
India's millennials saw the actor in lighter, comedy dramas&#160;like "Taxi No. 9211"&#160;where he played a taxi driver who wrecks havoc in a rich man's life after accidentally coming in possession of the key to the latter's wealth. Still, one of his most memorable roles will be that of underworld boss&#160;Uday Shetty in the 2007 movie "Welcome."&#160;His iconic dialogue&#160;from the movie are often meme-d and casually repeated among groups of friends.
Patekar was the recipient of three National Film Awards, several Indian Cinema Awards and state awards. He also received a Padma Shree, India's fourth-highest civilian honor.&#160;
In 2018, during India's #MeToo movement, an actress accused Patekar of sexually harassing her on the sets of a movie in 2008.
The actress alleged that he was present on the sets of song which did not feature him and tried to physically get close to her under the pretext of teaching&#160;dance moves.
Authorities had launched a formal investigation into the claims, which Patekar consistently denied. The probe was closed approximately a year later due to the lack of sufficient evidence.&#160;
Skip next section India denies bias after Musk's 'oligarchs' jab, says satellite rules 'fair' 10/08/2026 October 8, 2026 India denies bias after Musk's 'oligarchs' jab, says satellite rules 'fair'
Musk has been at odds with India's telecom leaders previously too Image: Evelyn Hockstein/REUTERS India on Thursday defended its satellite internet approval process, hours after US tech mogul Elon Musk accused unnamed "oligarchs" of blocking Starlink's launch in the country.&#160;
In a post on X, Musk had said "certain oligarchs in order to maintain their monopolistic chokehold on the Indian people," were blocking Starlink from entering the Indian market.&#160;&#160;
"You can guess who they are... This is a crime against the people of India!" he added.
India's telecom market is currently dominated by billionaires Mukesh Ambani's Reliance Jio and Sunil Mittal's Airtel. Both their satellite internet ventures, along with Musk's Starlink, have secured early approvals in India but are awaiting spectrum assignments.&#160;
For Starlink, this wait has stretched on for four years.&#160;
"The suggestion that the ‌framework ​or its application is unfair or discriminatory is baseless and misconceived," officials&#160;said in a statement.&#160;"Security assessment as part of this process is currently under way in respect of all the three licensees. As and when this process is completed for a licensed entity, it may proceed to the next step," the statement read.
Musk has previously too been at odds with Mittal and Ambani over how India should assign spectrum reach.
FYI: spectrum refers to the invisible radio frequencies which enable mobile calls, text messages, and data transmission.
For Musk's Starlink, at stake is access to a satellite broadband market which Deloitte forecasts will be worth $1.9 billion by 2030.
Starlink's India saga&#160;also features a layer of India-US diplomacy. Washington has pushed for greater access for Starlink as a goodwill marker in trade talks with countries burdened with President Donald Trump's tariffs. All of this while New Delhi and Washington are yet to finalize a trade deal since reaching an interim framework in February.&#160;
Skip next section Indian workers who built US consulate in Milan agree to record payout 10/08/2026 October 8, 2026 Indian workers who built US consulate in Milan agree to record payout
In all, nearly 480 workers can expect payouts with some 300 still working on the site Image: Luca Bruno/AP Photo/picture alliance Foreign workers, mostly Indian, building a vast new US consulate compound in Milan, Italy, signed agreements on Wednesday securing payouts averaging tens of thousands of euros each.
The payout is over alleged labor abuses while working for&#160;an American construction company.&#160;
Over 60 laborers signed the deal to receive their share of a €31 million (nearly $35 million) settlement with Alabama-based Caddell Construction. Prosecutors say this is the largest-ever settlement over labor exploitation in Europe.&#160;
The payouts include unpaid overtime, reimbursement for housing illegally deducted from their pay and other hefty damages sought by the prosecutors.&#160;
Dilip Ram, a 43-year-old mason from India who worked at the Milan site for over two years, said that the money would go a long way to help his family of five in the Indian state of Bihar.&#160;
The settlement is not an admission of liability, the construction company said Image: Antonio Calanni/AP Photo/picture alliance
"Things are better now than before …we get more rest now,"&#160;Ram said, after signing the deal. "This has led to a lot of change. This settlement will be good. Because the workers had troubles, that’s why this settlement has been applied."
Prosecutors alleged that workers were paid less than $2 (€1.8) an hour, were illegally docked money for housing and denied overtime for working up to 10 hours a day, six days a week. They were reportedly also subjected to threats and not given proper medical care when injured on the job.&#160;
While most of the workers were from India, others were from Nigeria, Kenya and Bangladesh.
The Indian workers reported having to pay&#160;€5,000 ($5,600) to recruiters to get the jobs. However, the current settlement could not&#160;address abuses in India as that is out of its jurisdiction.
Skip next section India seeks update from Moscow on Indian nationals working with Russian military 10/08/2026 October 8, 2026 India seeks update from Moscow on Indian nationals working with Russian military
India has sought an update from Russian authorities on the whereabouts of 69 Indian nationals reportedly working for the Russian military, Indian Foreign Ministry spokesperson Randhir Jaiswal told reporters on Wednesday evening.&#160;
Jaiswal said this during a routine media briefing. The ministry has been consistently urging Moscow to repatriate all Indian nationals serving in the Russian military since it found out that some 259 people had been recruited into the&#160;armed forces since the war in Ukraine began.&#160;
"Of these, through our efforts, so far, 139 Indian nationals have seen early discharge from the Russian Army," Jaiswal said.&#160;
"We are following up with the Russian authorities for updates on the whereabouts of 69 Indian nationals who are reportedly with the Russian Army," he added.&#160;
51 Indians have died so far while fighting for Russia on the front lines of Ukraine, according to Indian media.
Jaiswal warned against the "grave danger" of taking up jobs in the Russian military, saying "I once again want to urge that Indian nationals must stay away from offers being made to join the Russian Army."&#160;
